## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## How each report was placed

1. **One report, one row.** The export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice. They are
   counted once, by id.
2. **One-day moves first.** The desk already read some reports as a one-day move
   (`desk-notes.json`). Their bodies give a date one day off, an invite sent before the booking, or
   one meeting on two days in two time zones. That is not a day/month swap, and the parser fix
   will not touch it.
3. **The rest by tenant, not by browser.** `causes.ts` places a report by its tenant's locale and
   booking form. A report sent through the form of a `booking-v3` tenant is `hint`: the form tells
   people to type month first, then reads the date day first. Everything else from a day-first
   tenant is `parser`.

The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The column "intake tag wrong" shows how
often that guess put a report under the wrong problem.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and nobody reads
the body. All three problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is
wrong"), so all three landed in #4471. The incident page shows `hint` as a second cause with no
ticket of its own, and footnotes the one-day moves under `parser`.

## What to do

- Keep #4471 for the parser. Do not mark the others fixed when its fix ships.
- `hint`: change the `booking-v3` date hint to match how the form reads the date.
- `shift`: find where invite dates cross a time zone; it is not the parser.
- Merge on cause, not subject: [DedupeExplorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) already shows that.

